    {
      "id": "RTC-03",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-regular-shift-multiplicity",
      "title": "Why each regular shifted coordinate occurs just once",
      "hypotheses": "With the same regular parameter and gap, the averaging maps separate smooth tensors for the defining and dual defining representations, including the required reverse and second regular shifts.",
      "statement": "A second lowering has a Casimir scalar different from the scalar required of a trace-zero double tensor. Contraction thus detects every intertwiner from π to the defining tensor with πs. Schur’s lemma bounds that space by one dimension. Tensor adjunction and joint separation give a direct sum with one copy of each regular model.",
      "definitions": [
        "The quadratic Casimir scalar is the sum of squares of the Harish-Chandra coordinates minus a fixed rank constant.",
        "Contraction on V⊗V* is the trace. The fixed reverse inclusion and a variable forward map yield an endomorphism of the shifted model after contraction."
      ],
      "dependencies": [
        "RTC-02"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "normalization-regular-second-shift"
      ],
